##
    Reading a variable before it has been given a value.

    This program does not compile. Every local must be proved to hold a value on every path
    that reaches it, which is the other half of doing without null: there is no "unset" state
    for a program to stumble into.
##

# Nothing here is run, so nothing reads what it declares and nothing keeps what it
# works out. That is the shape of every negative, and not the lesson of this one.
# ignore PC0409 in file
# ignore PC0410 in file

shared model Program

    ##
        A shared field belongs to the type rather than to any instance, so no constructor runs
        that could fill it in and the initializer is the only place a value can come from.

        Only for a type that has no value of its own. A primitive starts at its own zero and is
        left alone below on purpose — 'counted' is not a mistake. A set or a function has no
        such zero, and one left alone would hold the null the language says it does not have,
        reached by the reader as a sentence about the wrong thing or as a crash from underneath
        the language.
    ##
    delegate() announce;
    integer[] gathered;
    integer counted;

    function Main(boolean flag)
        # Never assigned at all.
        integer total;
        Console.WriteLine(total);

        # Assigned on one branch only, so the join does not carry it.
        integer chosen;

        if flag
            chosen = 1;
        end if

        Console.WriteLine(chosen);

        # A try body may stop anywhere, so the catch cannot rely on what it assigned.
        integer attempted;

        try
            attempted = 1;
        catch Exception problem
            Console.WriteLine(attempted);
        end try

        # A constant must be given its value where it is declared.
        constant integer Limit;
    end function
end model
